        "overview": "JAYCEE’s round is built on status. From the start, he frames FENDRY as beneath him: amateur skill, not roster level, a fan, a “sweet boy,” and someone lucky to be in the battle. He repeatedly makes the point that he should be in the studio rather than wasting time in this clash.\n\nHis best material comes when he gets specific. The reference to **JM already having FENDRY on ropes** gives the round a battle-history angle. The line about FENDRY sending him a friendly text before the clash also works because it paints FENDRY as intimidated behind the scenes. He also has good PenGame references around DUSK, DEENO, ZEN in the final, PASSIVE beats, and PenGame North.\n\nThe issue is that JAYCEE’s round is long and sometimes overextended. He has a lot of energy and confidence, but the writing becomes repetitive around “you’re a fan,” “you’re beneath me,” and “I’ll fight you.” Some of the second half feels more like dominance talk than tight punch writing.\n\nStill, JAYCEE has a strong performance presence, and his delivery gives the round momentum. His strongest case is that he looked more comfortable and more aggressive from the jump.",
