        "performer": "KANDI",
        "overview": "KANDI brings the type of madness people expect from him. His first round has high energy, quick aggression and some memorable phrasing. The 'target acquired / contract to eliminate' opening gives the round an assassin/video-game tone, and he attacks RA'SIAH's masculinity, sexuality, family, children, Rosa Parks line and toughness.\n\nKANDI's best moments come when his flow catches a rhythm. The 'in out, in out' section and the 'Springtown / kingpin / win win / bling bling' run show why he gets crowd reaction. He has shock value, confidence and the ability to make the room react even when the material is messy.\n\nHis second round has flashes of writing, especially the K-ANDI name breakdown, the 'best in the ends' section, the mum/element lines, the barber/sheep/llama scheme, and the closet bar. But the round becomes less focused and loses shape compared with RA'SIAH's second.\n\nHis third round is weaker because KANDI admits he originally wrote it for PRYNLEE and then decided to use it here. That makes the material feel less tailored. He still lands moments, especially the Morgz bar, the benefits/transport/landlord angle, and the 'leave the music alone' closer, but it is not enough to beat RA'SIAH's cleaner third.\n\nKANDI's strength is chaos and shock value. His weakness is structure. In this battle, RA'SIAH had the cleaner writing and the clearer argument.",
