        "performer": "RA’SIAH",
        "overview": "RA’SIAH comes out strong and arguably takes round one. His first round is high-energy and technically confident. He attacks PASSIVE’s producer identity, saying he should stick to making beats because his bars are weak. He criticises PASSIVE for trying to sound like Skepta and D Double E, calls him a copycat, says he does not rate him as an opponent, and uses production terms like FL Studio, Pro Tools, mix, master, vocals and Logic to make the angle fit.\n\nHis second round is more character-focused. He attacks PASSIVE’s old “Spider” name, calling it corny and unoriginal, then builds a full Spider-Man scheme: Peter Parker, Dr Octavius, Green Goblin, Mary Jane and hero/villain framing. He also attacks PASSIVE’s ex-girl and flips PASSIVE’s name into **M-A-S-S-I-V-E**. This round has strong ideas, but it becomes a bit long and sex-angle heavy.\n\nHis third round has good moments but does not quite close hard enough. RA’SIAH opens with confidence, says PASSIVE is not on his level, brings a mum angle from a Peckham encounter, attacks PASSIVE’s style as basic, says ZEN showed him levels, and uses a run of cartoon/comedy comparisons: Dobby, Ben 10, Noddy, Timmy Turner and Jimmy Neutron. He closes with a strong mum/beat/bedroom angle, but the judges feel he tails off compared with PASSIVE.\n\nRA’SIAH’s strength is flow and delivery. His issue is round balance: he starts hot, but later rounds lean into similar sexual/family angles instead of widening the attack enough.",
